  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Friday)\nIssued at 1210 PM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nRain chances continue through much of the day Saturday across far\neastern Oklahoma and western Arkansas, while drier conditions are\nforecast across the western half of the CWA. Cloudy and rainy\nconditions will keep temperatures down again on Saturday as highs\nwill trend near 70 for most of the area Saturday afternoon.\nGenerally light amounts can be expected again on Saturday with\nisolated pockets up to a half inch possible across western\nArkansas. Weak mid level ridging builds in behind the exiting wave\non Sunday, providing a brief break in the rain chances. Afternoon\nhighs will rebound as well into the upper 70s under partly cloudy\nskies.\n\nAttention will then be on the main impact maker through this\nforecast period as a dynamic upper level system moves into the\narea on Monday. A more potent upper level trough will move on\nshore the west coast over the weekend and eject out over the\nPlains during the day Monday. At the same time, a surface low will\ndevelop and deepen over the Central Plains before lifting\nnortheastward into the Upper Mississippi Valley late in the day\nMonday, with an attendant cold front stretching southwestward from\nthe surface low. Increasing southerly flow, with surface wind \ngusts around 30 to 40 mph possible Monday afternoon, will bring \nample low level moisture into the local region by Monday \nafternoon. Scattered showers are likely to be ongoing Monday\nmorning within a strong warm advection region noted by a 40 to\n50 knot 850hPa jet. How these showers evolve will likely play a\nrole in the strong to severe potential later in the day. Current\nconsensus is that an increasing risk for strong to severe\nthunderstorms will become possible by mid afternoon Monday ahead\nof the southeastward moving cold front. Sufficient instability,\nMUCAPE around 1000J/kg, along with bulk shear values around 50 \nknots will support both supercell and more organized linear\nstructures along the strongly forced cold front. Damaging wind\ngusts look to be the main threat with the stronger storms as\nrelatively weak mid level lapse rates don't look supportive of \nlarge hail at this point. A tornado threat could also evolve for a\nshort time at the onset of stronger storms as forecast hodographs\nshow sufficient turning of low level winds ahead of the front. \nCurrent timing suggests that the most notable threat will evolve \nacross mainly central Oklahoma, into parts of eastern Oklahoma \ngenerally along and west of a Bartlesville to McAlester to Hugo \nline. Instability is forecast to drop off rapidly after sunset, \nand thus the more potent severe threat will wane into the evening \nhours as the front pushes southeastward.\n\nIn addition to the severe threat, the increasing moisture will\nprovide anomalously high PWAT values. Thus, areas of flash\nflooding could evolve over more urban or flood prone areas as\nhigher rain rates occur with the stronger thunderstorms. The rest\nof the forecast period will be noted by mainly dry conditions with\ncooler, more normal early November temperatures on tap in the wake\nof the front. Widespread lows in the 40s are likely for several\ndays, while some locations across the north drop into the 30s at\ntimes. \n\nBowlan",
